Home is behind me.
A bucolic chimera, the American Midwest. Small towns exist in isolation, separated by corn and cows and calloused fingers. Water towers scar the sky, baptismal fonts supported by bones of iron and emblazoned with the epitaphic date of some ancient athletic victory. The air is heavy and claustrophobic, a sepulchral sigh ushered out from railroad crossings and gas station marquees.
“Welcome,” says the diner waitress, weeping anemic coffee. “Don’t you dare start thinking.”
Drive on through. And so I have.
Scenery passes at a speed I’ve forgotten. The hum of poor machinery fills my skull, pushing from me all my senses and leaving in their wake a deep and commanding catatonia. I blink and maybe I cross a continent. The world materializes as though from behind frosted glass, too blurred and too far for its features to converge into an identity. The hand of a clock, static. Appearing the same from any perspective. The narrator is me and I am here, but I suppose that I could be anywhere. 
My keychain, jingling; the arrhythmic leitmotif of a dog on linoleum. An elderly flower girl clatters forward in her wheelchair and she is the sound of my car’s battered motor. A shrill ringing, barely audible--a vehicle accelerating through space or a blow to the head.
I am ten years old, playing with my brother on our bunk bed. We are imagining ourselves the characters of a light gun arcade game. I play the villains. All of them, perpetually. I honor the sanctity of my station, presenting myself as a miscellany of opponents with varied patterns of attack. I am deliberate in counting how many “hits” I score, comparing them against the “deaths” that I suffer. Whenever the ratio skews in my favor, I linger. I slow and become more predictable. I give my brother the chance to defeat ostensibly tougher enemies with ease.
At one point we swap bunks and I attack from below. My brother slips, falls. Crashes to the ground like a mortar shell. The welt on his head appears immediately. He is six years old and cries as loud as he can. Our father throws open the door. Seeing my brother sobbing on the floor, he strikes me across the face so hard that my orthodontic retainer flies from my mouth.
I blink in the present. Awareness returns.
Contrary to my original assertion, I couldn’t be anywhere else at all.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Burns, 120 mi.

I am driving and I hate cars. Dressed up in dishonest curves, veins bulging with gold that burns. An archaic design, atrophied, propped up like a showroom mannequin. A carcass as a commodity.
There has to be a better way to move people around. Fuck, though, wouldn’t teleportation be terrifying? Complete molecular deconstruction. Literal death. In the hurried momentum of that final instant, with air rushing forward to fill the vacuum occupied only moments before by every piece of myself, wouldn’t I try to scream out? 
“Wait, stop! This isn’t right!”
Then a thousand miles away there stands a man who is built of all the same materials--and not merely of the same materials, but with those components arranged in such a particular manner as to perfectly duplicate all my silent dreams and unquantifiable fears. He stretches, checks the time, and goes to grab a latte before heading into the office.
There has to be a better way to move people around.
I don’t actually hate driving. My foot finds the pedal and presses downward, eager; on a dry autumn leaf or enemy soldier’s throat. There is no crunch or crack, though. My aural reward is a crescendo, as quick as I want, the whirring of a steel skeleton. Dials lean toward high noon and then fall toward evening. I could be weightless, the arc held fast in my fists the only anchor that tethers me to the world whipping past. Behind me, beneath me, nothing.
The sky is sharp and smooth, curved glass stretching the full breadth of my vision. Arms of clear fire reach down and paint the landscape. The trees are alight. I can hear them vibrating, all the potential energy of a new world waiting for a cue. I am jealous, in a way. Those verdant pillars will remain as torches forever, while we are fated to flicker like matches. Our humming friction bursting out into the wide embrace of the azure. To be caught by nothing. Children tripping with arms outstretched.
Live in the moment. That’s the dream of a generation, isn’t it? The mantra bleated from pop stars and pill pushers. Don’t fear for the future; don’t pine for the past. Nothing matters but the moment. Suspended forever in a vacuum of the heart, a world devoid of context or weight or meaning.
No regrets.
What’s the fucking point of that? Someday the fragile seal of memory will burst and nothing will remain to tie together those moments. But with regret there is something solid, a line giving shape to otherwise disparate vertices.
I don’t want to live like a match. I want what they have, those trees, the circular jewelry of their actual bodies defined by everything that came before.
But then what? Fate waits to direct the wayward away, down roads that rot beneath their patrons. Voyagers become wanderers, stranded in broad tracts of fog and dust.
Years ago a friend came to me with a tip. Their neighbor was offloading an assortment of furniture for free. I had no means to transport a couch across town, but a television stand--a stage for an appliance I’d left resting on the floor like a dragon’s hoard--was both feasible and worth the effort.
I remember heading up the stairs to that apartment, my feet pushing me beneath an overhang and into a gloom of cobwebs. They stretched toward me like fibrous sinew, starved and grasping at fascination. They clung to my face, my arms. I reached to brush them away. They wrapped up my lungs. They wanted me to stay.
My knock echoed, became a voice barking in a language I did not understand. The door opened, a click and a hiss. A slender form pushed itself outward from the rich darkness beyond. Emaciated arms; purple wrists; ribs hidden by clothing but visible beneath skin stretched tight by neglect. A pair of dull eyes that never once met my own.
We exchanged words, of course, but for the life of me I cannot recall them. I remember only the feeling of it. The anxiety of fumbling through a conversation; of searching for words as I searched for an exit; of trying with every breath to fend off the covetous silken filigree that fell upon us like wan ghosts.
And then came the addendum.
“Is there anything else you want?”
A question. A suggestion. An invitation. The shape of a body as it offers itself.
“Wait!” I wanted to shout. “Stop! This isn’t right!”
But then what?
People stand, but so do mannequins. What stood in that doorway resembled the former in shape alone. This was not something capable of thought or action. Nothing in the world had ever been affected by the ideas or intentions of this object. This was something upon which other people visited their own exertions.
What could I do for this receptacle?
I left them stranded in the fog and dust.
Live in the moment, kiddo.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Burns, 80 mi.

We made this journey once before, my wife and I.
There’s something special about driving toward the ocean. Concrete notions take on abstract shapes. The places from which we leave, the reasons for which we travel, the companions with whom the hours are kept; these become irrelevant. With the breadth of a nation in the rearview mirror and a horizon of parallel lines hanging beyond the windshield, it feels like going home. 
“I’m coming,” we say, remembering our primordial mother.
My family and I vacationed along the east coast years before my wife and I made our own excursion to the ocean. My parents selected our destinations with care, wanting to treat my brother and I to a change of scenery while still protecting us from the sweeping blades of some great tourism combine. I’d seen a lot of the Atlantic by the time my wife and I headed toward the Pacific, and even so I hadn’t really been prepared.
The Atlantic ocean feels like a void. Stand on the sand and reach out; the dust of another continent hangs in the air. A phantom pain. The severed limb remembers its partner. The ocean regrets itself, an existence made manifest by divorce.
But our country’s other ocean? The first time I walked out into the foam and wind--the wet beach crumbling between my toes, soft notes of green and grey on my tongue--I knew that I’d found hallowed ground without fear in its heart. 
“I am what belongs,” said the water. 
I looked back at my wife. Her eyes said the same.
She is … well, what can I say? What pieces do I pick out and shuffle around, grouping by similarity where applicable? What are the headers? Enticing. Enervating. Enraging.
She is the one I chose to marry. Is there anything more that I need to say?
I remember when we met.
The professor of art history at my college had added to the curriculum the requirement that every student attend five separate live performances during the semester, each exhibiting a different medium, with the understanding that we would document our impressions for eventual administrative appraisal. I sought out only the cheapest venues and shortest engagements, having little interest in the particular sort of broader artistic experiences that the assignment was intended to provide.
One evening a handful of local punk bands were hosted by a divey little Robert Rodriguez set piece near my apartment. None of the music aligned with my personal taste, but I realized that if I hated it beyond reconciliation I could simply walk home and chronicle (accurately or otherwise) how all of the bands had lacked talent in the same generic capacity. Whoever ended up grading my paper wouldn’t exactly be able to argue my claims.
In the end I didn’t pay attention to more than a third of the first band’s set. I forgot about writing the paper altogether.
I met her.
Our relationship was never easy, but we made our way through. 
I don’t mean to say that we leapt from stone to stone, clinging to each other in an attempt to maintain balance on a narrow foundation even as our feet dangled off the curved edges and into the black water beyond. No. We dove in deep, swimming in defiance even as the dark throat of some abyssal kraken opened up far greater depths below us.
But from time to time we found our stones.
That’s what set us driving toward the beach. 
I will never forget it. The clouds above us overflowing with sunlight, spilling white and molten promises. We reached up, the light pooling in our hands and pouring into our eyes. The ground was the air and our arms were pillars. We were up and we were floating, hanging inverted above a dream of the sky. 
The radio faded to static as we drifted.
I’m probably beyond the reach of my preferred FM stations by now, but for this trip I’ve already ensured that the radio is off. I don’t want it. Not now. I don’t need distractions.
I am driving to meet my wife. 

Burns, 30 mi.

The road tilts downward and the pages of the country open up before me. Neither a flyleaf, the verso and recto are laden with heavy green lettering. The typeface rises off the pages. Dried ink, organic and imprecise. The language, long studied but forever impenetrable, might speak of kings or gods or vultures. Neglect scars the road, a spine with dangerously frayed binding. I am struck by the ephemerality of it, the delicate leaves of the narrative. A strong wind could pass through at any moment and scatter it all to dust. 
I pass through.
Heaven has turned and the sun is lower. The day dies as I watch. A gradient of pink and orange blazes before me; above me, a regal blue. And the gleaming grin of the moon, as narrow and cryptic as it ever was.
“You don’t need to worry,” it lies. “This is for the best. I know what you need.”
Destruction is simple. Uninspired, even. Any stupid fuck can beat down a door or a castle or a heart. Finesse is not requisite for force. All ashlar gives way, eventually. Even worms may eat the world.
“Look!” they say, holding aloft a severed head or notched bedpost. “I have conquered.”
Nothing.
We perceive power in a destroyer. A tower of maggots.
To create is something else altogether. Empty space must be filled. Resolve made manifest, where before there was void.
But there is another act, a tertiary possibility when humans put to work their dirty, shaking hands. It is intoxicating. It is a secret.
The moribund halo of celestial radiation is sinking now, falling beneath the horizon as creation continues spinning. Left to die alone below the straight line of the Earth. And below the straight line of a road. Identical stone columns rise on either side of my own route, holding up a bridge. The walls of a mausoleum. I hold my breath, preparing to pass through.
I learned this little ritual during the early years of my youth, but despite my best attempts I cannot recall what it is supposed to mean. Something about spirits, I think. They skulk in the dark and hollow spaces that we forget to sweep or wash, coalescing, sticking together with gossamer strands of spectral mischief. Until some hapless wanderer stumbles past. Then they detach from each other like burrs from a stalk, waiting to be inhaled so that they may subsume their new host.
With full lungs and a closed mouth I pass beneath the bridge.
I need this to be perfect.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Silver Lake, 110 mi.

A bridge is, in a way, a promise. Or an apology. Probably the latter. 
“I’m sorry,” comes the voice from beneath the keystone, ostensibly sound and yet shaking by design with every passing vehicle. “I could think of nothing better.” 
The sky slowly fills with concrete and steel. 
One of my favorite means of escape during my youth--long before I ever knew why a person might want to escape--was a computer game, a simulation that tasked the player with managing an assortment of amusement parks. It offered dozens of objectives in a variety of locales, but even as I progressed from one scenario to the next I would find myself returning to the file I’d saved after completing the very first level. Here I had purchased all of the available land and paid off all outstanding debts. My guests were happy. The park very nearly ran itself.
But my desire for manufactured conquest could not be sated. I continued building long after there was any need. Walkways began to ascend upward, entwining themselves around the steel anatomy of so many attractions and rides. The ground gradually disappeared from view, obscured by the woven tapestry that choked the air and defied the sun.
That is the promise made by a bridge. The promise that begets the apology. Out here, with only a narrow road leading me toward the wide breadth of an ocean, it is difficult to imagine such a future. But I still manage it. 
The sun has perished at last, fallen from sight as the weight of the cosmos forces one final, wistful breath from those roiling lungs. Only its myriad arms remain visible, cast upward and frozen; the grimace of death. Reaching toward or warding off the slender smile of the moon. The wind wakes. The trees whisper, calming their children with stories and songs. Tall shadows begin to blear, a funhouse mirror consumed by the growing darkness.
I notice clouds amassing behind me.
Dammit.
Driving in the rain is not fun. It is claustrophobia. It is a wall of water, omnipresent like the eyes of God. It is a hidden passenger and their own lurking counterpart. A hope that the wall will move when we push. A fear that it will not.
It is still not as awful as walking in the rain.
I’ve done enough of that. I don’t need to be reminded of gaunt and crooked street lights; aphotic weeds bending without nourishment, vomiting their bile onto the slick concrete. I don’t need to be reminded of desolate avenues, emptied by migration or pandemic or the hyperbolic hunger of our nightmare beasts. I don’t need to be reminded of a saturating chill that clings like mold, that eats like rot, that pushes inward with sick intention and violates our deepest places.
“Wait. Stop.” The words have the texture of dirt. “This isn’t right.”
The rain comes anyway.
Shit.
I need this to be perfect.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Silver Lake, 70 mi.

I ended up in an accident about four months after getting my driver’s license. Nothing severe. The icy winter of northern Illinois simply proved more than my inexperience could handle. I found myself drifting without traction, off the road and into a bank of snow.
Awful and duplicitous, a Midwestern winter cannot be trusted. A teenage boy harboring unrequited affections. 
“I just want to be friends,” says the glittering snow, pure and white as it falls from the sky. “I want you to be happy, no matter what. And I know you better than anyone.”
He gives gifts and attention. For a time we enjoy ourselves. The world is soft and unblemished. A postcard time capsule, a honeyed reverie. Purple skies; red noses; ivory breath that billows and swirls. We have the chance to create clouds, a brief and private sky.
But he remains and remains and remains, unchanged in his own mind even as his true intentions become obvious.
“Why,” he demands of his mirror, practicing that silver smile, “don’t they love me. No one will ever be as loyal!”
Salt renders his alabaster facade ineffectual. Grey slush fills the streets in its stead, splattering out from under spinning tires and squelching with every footfall.
“Think of all I’ve sacrificed! The nice guy, he never gets what he wants.”
The diesel groan of a plow echoes through the neighborhood, heralding the arrival of those irregular, sloping mounds. We drive on through, vehicular exhaust accumulating in grimy black stains.
“I’m doing this all for you.”
Wind howls down. Frigid fury. The soiled alluvium grows hard, capped now by a sparkling mockery of gemstone brilliance.
“I am your friend!”
Which is how I found myself unable to control my car, skidding helplessly into a snowbank even as I noticed another driver rendered similarly destitute on the opposite side of the road. A delivery truck driver, he let me spend the next hour in the cabin of his vehicle--his truck being a safer place of refuge, in the event that yet another hapless driver succumbed to the beguiling weather. 
Very little of our conversation now remains for me to recollect, but I do remember discussing our mutual, foiled itineraries. More specifically, I remember his response to my explanation that I had intended to visit my then-girlfriend. Over a hundred miles separated our parents’ houses, making it difficult for me to see her very often. The recent commencement of Christmas vacation meant that I could visit for longer than a weekend. The intensity of youth only served to amplify my desire to make use of this time. 
“Oh,” was his response, so gentle in its sincerity. “You were going home.”
I was too young to understand.
At least, not in the way that I understand it now.
The rain falls harder now, the gentle footsteps of a mourning sky replaced by a roaring locomotive--by the rhythmic barking of steel on steel as a heartbroken darling weeps through a window, carried further and further from home along the broad tracks of the sky. Windshield wipers beat against this percussion, matching it in neither tempo nor fervor. 
I realize that I’ve had my jaw clenched. For how long?
And there is a change. I feel it. Behind me, mostly, but also above and below and beside. I can’t see it. Or I just don’t want to. But I know this feeling. 
Like scattered limbs pulled together by a sigh borne on the breath of awe; like splintered nerves reaching across the gaping chasm of a fresh and flowing wound; like crooked necks bending to touch the stony hands of worship. The clouds behind me boil and swell, grotesque in their fertility. They darken, burnt black by some internal fire, leaving behind nothing more than crumbling charcoal as they swallow the stars. They pursue, angling downward, stretching out long and hungry tendrils, at once grasping and pushing.
“Wait!” The word comes like a discharge, shorter and louder than expected. 
“Stop!” Another small explosion triggered by the first; collateral damage.
“This isn’t right!”
I need this to be perfect.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Silver Lake, 20 mi.

The shape of the earth has changed. No longer gentle or gradual in its declination, it rises and falls with erratic inconsistency; my car groans in protest with every sudden incline. The road, now doubtful of the truest path to the coast, winds and weaves between precipitous slopes, many bearing a single face of angry red stone--a scar left in the wake of a serrated blade, or the gluttonous expansion of an unwilling body. The foliage is thicker, too, as though the compression of the ground below pushed all the verdance of a countryside upward at once.
I’ve been driving all this time atop the frame of a cosmic vehicle, and have just now reached the point of impact where it collided with another.
Isn’t it funny that such a common thing--something so integral to the very infrastructure of our culture--can be so easily used as a literary device to invoke a dire fate? 
It’s funny, too, how so much of what transpires in our actual lives happens beyond the pale of narrative believability. It must be diluted and sanitized as it makes the transition from empirical circumstance to dramatic prose.
For example: I could talk about the dire fate of vehicular origin that chanced to change the life of a friend, how his father drove home from a bar one night in an angry and drunken stupor and, in the process, lost his life. It’s not the truth, of course, but it fits a familiar pattern and is easy on the palette. Sweet sorrow, tinged with the faint herbal notes of perceived moral recompense. Comfort food.
But like I said, that isn’t what happened. Instead, his father made the responsible decision and called a friend for a ride. And then tripped in his own garage; hit his head on the sharp corner of a concrete stair; and, without anyone to find him until he missed work on Monday, left this world.
What kind of taste that does leave behind?
I look up into the mirror suspended above my head, into a panoptic vault of darkness. The clouds have not slackened in their pursuit. I can feel them, thick and suffocating. Corporeal intent, corpuscular malice. My muscles threaten to cramp as I press harder on the accelerator.
Wait, where was I?
Dire fates.
Fuck, and isn’t it arrogant to make an attempt at avenging anyone else’s?
“I want to hurt the people who hurt you.”
What a selfish fucking notion. Those experiences are theirs. They are the origin along all axes, the bright yellow star in the heliocentric astronomy of personal horror. Everything else is simply in orbit, points plotted by a function built around all that has befallen. We have no right to take from them their place in the center of those Copernican mathematics. We don’t get to take that for which they paid in blood and make it about us.
So we cannot avenge. We can only bear witness. We can keep our gaze fixed on the burning star in the midst of that awful expanse, to watch the patterns of every celestial body carving a path in the deepness of that sky. Holding out our arms, we can still say and mean those very important words.
“I love you.”
And not in spite of that atrocity. And not because of that atrocity. It is simply a piece of what they are, one more among all the others. One more piece for us to love. One more piece for us to hate.
It is black outside my car. Not dark, but black.
The king of all kings, personified in myth as sky and thunder incarnate, worshipped by every creature kneeling in abject servitude beneath the sun; even he is afraid of the night. It would have been nice if the old bastard had told us why.
Headlights glare, painting nothing beyond the slick asphalt a yard or so ahead of my advance. That road may as well be an ocean, the light a beacon. Shining as a warning even where sailors have long since ceased to travel, or as a spurious reflection of the Milky Way’s own diaphanous brushwork.
I am nowhere, following a route to which I am all but blind. Mere inches of glass and metal separate me from falling down or up or out, away into a storm that can never end. Into the iron embrace of the thing that follows. It is a thing, now. The clouds are gone, replaced by a thing that gallops with a rattling stride and leers from eyes like the beaks of crows. It hungers. I can feel that insatiable want. My muscles tense further. I lean forward, head pounding, neck throbbing, knuckles white on the wheel.
I am trying.
I need this to be perfect.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Pacific Beach, 90 mi.

A sharp curve races toward me, scrambling with the sudden staccato motion of an insect fleeing luminescence. I pull hard on the wheel. To the left, right? My foot never leaves the accelerator. Tires fail to find purchase. The world whirls. Or at least, I assume as much. Some senses agree: My body leans, inertia demanding a greater sacrifice for my transgressions. But others dissent: The splash of blurred monochrome caught between the beams of light before me does not look any different in this moment than it did in the last. I might not even have turned at all.
The thing behind me howls, battering the sides of my car. I pull my arms in tight.
This is what we’ve evolved for, isn’t it? This is how the human, with her weak eyes and dull teeth and paltry strength, was able to survive in the darkness of primitive earth. By running.
Notions of speed sure as shit didn’t have anything to do with any of it. Not a chance. No, she simply endured. She ran down her prey across a vast expanse of wilderness, beneath sun and moonlight alike, always in steady pursuance. She ran until her prey tired and foundered, pushed far beyond any capacity for recovery. Speed means nothing for a creature lying face down in the dirt with white eyes and a foaming mouth.
Then she carried the carcass back home.
Always running.
From our problems, too.
At one point during middle school my homeroom teacher came upon a woefully misguided idea for a group activity, intended to … actually, I don’t know. I don’t think we ever received any input regarding what this endeavor was supposed to teach us. For better and worse, our instructions for an afternoon expected us to write “get well” cards for terminally ill children at a nearby hospital. Except, of course, these children were never going to get well, since they were fated to die in the very near future.
I was immediately struck, even at that age, by the maudlin pageantry of it. A host of naive sixth graders, reared in upper-middle class security and protected from having any genuine perspective, would only ever be able to craft a catalog of hollow platitudes. It made me very nearly physically angry, watching classmates of mine draw balloons on construction paper. What did any of us know of that world? 
Balloons.
Live in the moment.
And so I tried to search for some meaning, to scrawl something on my empty green sheet of paper that might carry even a modicum of actual value for someone whose body was rapidly dying. Retrospectively, of course, my behavior was every bit as audaciously selfish as the original assignment was insipid, but at the time it seemed like the right thing to do. 
I didn’t draw any balloons.
The cards were collected and reviewed. Mine was immediately singled out as not simply unacceptable but wholly and unabashedly offensive. Blame for this misdeed fell squarely upon two of my classmates--the ones whom anyone would suspect, the ones who had over the years built for themselves the classic reputations of irreverent agitators. Having been so proud of my righteous or self-righteous defiance mere moments before, I found myself unable to take ownership of this perceived wrongdoing. I gladly let them take the fall.
A proud heir to my biological legacy. A great runner.
Years later I visited that school and tracked down that teacher. In an embarrassing display of graceless penitence confessed to having written the full content of that card. I cannot now fathom what kind of closure I had sought, but it doesn’t really matter.
“So?” 
A monosyllabic response riding the steady gaze of apathy.
I did not find closure.
Having started running, I found myself unable to stop. Even once I wanted to.
Thick, sinuous limbs of indistinct shape grip my car. The chassis groans around me, threatening to buckle inward. I hammer the accelerator, desperate to escape. At any moment I might push through the floor and fall down into the molten core of whatever earth remains below me. The engine redlines, a great metallic whine rising in discordance with the ceaseless wailing of the predacious thing that follows.
My car cannot handle this abuse.
My car cannot give out on me.
I am trying. I am trying.
I need this to be perfect. 
I am driving to meet my wife.

Pacific Beach, 40 mi.

I have an uncle living somewhere in the vast cornstalk desert of Iowa. The road running past his residence was probably paved once, a long time ago, before being left in the care of a callous climate. Surmounting a small hill from the east, the broken asphalt dips down toward an uncertain horizon, disappearing into the veiled line where myotic sunlight flickers against the undulating contours of a pastoral landscape. And the only structure visible in all that space is my uncle’s house. He never married, having found love in the dozen or so canines to whom he gives free reign of his property. 
My high school girlfriend and I visited him once, spending just one evening in the finished attic of that little A-frame house. I know now that it had the potential to be one of the most terrifying nights of my life. No, that’s not accurate. The terror was more than a potential. What I know now is how much worse it still could have been.
For years I’ve feigned introspection, pretending that I cannot fathom what drove me to behave as I did. Was it the distance? Beneath a window like a black painting, the light from stars suspended lifetimes away serving as the only reminder of anything tangible. Was it the unfamiliarity? Beneath a window like a banner, the sight of an unfamiliar ceiling and scent of an unfamiliar tribe forcing my primal instincts to awaken. Was it the intimacy? Beneath a window like a mirror, a reflection of her closeness, her breath and touch and heat.
What a pathetic charade. As if I would have acted differently without any of that shit.
I didn’t pressure the hinges past their integrity, but, well, I still applied that pressure. I think about it often. How close I had come to fully realizing the worst version of myself.
What kept me from becoming an abuser? Cowardice? I didn't have any trouble assailing her with selfish persistence; “virtue” did nothing to reign in my urgency. And what kept her from becoming a victim? Luck? What meager good fortune, dating an only partially-developed night terror.
This is all that keeps any of us safe from dire fates.
A sudden change in pressure tugs at my neck. I realize that the rear windshield is gone, shattered. Making of me a comet with a twinkling tail, or an unlucky thief spilling his spoils as he flees from justice. The horrific thing pushes inward, quivering in lurid anticipation, ushering up from infinite depths a nightmare wail.
There is nothing I can do to stop it.
I want to stop it.
I am trying. I am trying. I am trying.
I need this to be perfect.
I am driving to meet my wife.

Pacific Beach, 15 mi.

A feeling begins to boil within me. I know it already and the body responds, hot with impurity. Tears well up into whiskey tumblers. Teeth grind along a gambler’s table. Fists clench tight to seal their vaults. It hurts and I cannot stop.
I’ve carried it all my life, this anger. I’ve fed and nurtured these embers, as though they might one day save me from freezing in the wilderness. 
What, the wilderness of emotional maturity?
There is a secret here, actually. About my ire. Nothing great, and nothing terrible. Just a sickness. A sad little disease.
And oh, how I’ve tried to trick myself into believing that this anger belongs. I act a fool. As though this somehow gives depth to my character, lending to me the allure of a brilliant and damaged artist. Or worse, as though this fire burns atop some righteous impetus. 
Fuck. Even that metaphor--comparing my toxic thoughts to something so often used as an analog for passion--is just disgusting.
It’s not a fucking fire. There are no redeeming qualities. My anger is not a dynamic and critical component of my persona. I am not driven to it by perceptions of injustice, nor by my own zealous dedication to anything that matters. No. It merely fills the empty space created by an absence of power.
It is the facade erected to disguise inadequacy.
It is the flailing of a petulant child.
Destruction is the vocation of the weak. 
Creation is the glory of gods. 
But what about that third performance? The secret deed, the one built upon both the other corners of the trinity?
Transformation.
To take something that already exists. To take something with a shape and purpose and identity all its own. To take that and strip away the surface and break down the components found within and then catalogue and categorize those separated pieces before placing each in the tidy little corresponding pile in order to facilitate the change that comes from subtraction and preservation and modification and addition until the thing that was before is now no longer and what remains is a manifestation of my own hungry vision.
Will overpowering will.
How awful and beautiful is that?
I want to be that awful. I want to be that beautiful.
I am not. I am not. I am not.
I am driving to be perfect.
I need to meet my wife.

Exit 55

The headlights die, the last creatures to leave this world. I drive onward and all of creation would shudder beneath my passing if any of it now remained. The thing rides with me, pushing with all the monstrous fury of a witch-hunt pyre. I still grip the wheel but I have no control. I turn left and am pushed forward. I turn right and am pushed forward. I scream and my throat is the moldering husk of a body broken upon Catherine’s wheel.
“Wait! Stop! This isn’t right!”
Then I am over a crest in the earth. There is a sudden deafening rush of wind and sand and light. Skidding to a halt, the ruined remnants of my vehicle are showered in grains of dirt. 
The silence that follows makes of me a statue. I cannot move. I cannot breathe. My heart has stopped.
I blink. My hands shake violently as I lift them from the wheel. I pull hard on the handle of the door, and after a moment of metallic lament I tumble onto the beach. The fine granules of sand shift smoothly beneath my weight. They fill my vision. I am drawn toward them. Into them. I dream of digging, of forcing my fingers deeper and deeper downward. I want to feel my body erode with every new stroke, until I become nothing more than grist of the shore. Until I am gone.
After a momentous exertion--I hear the muscles in my neck separating in protest--I tug my gaze upward. 
Dawn hums to life, cool and pale and blue. Light blooms lazily on the gentle contours of the coastline, on the sinuate mask of the water. A steady breeze blows, a white noise counterpoint to the husky murmur of the waves.
The ocean is just as I remember. Dark. Deep. Eternal. Spanning the full expanse of my vision. It could stretch outward forever, hanging in space above the curve of the Earth, flowing in perpetuity into the boundless infinite.
And here, resolute in the center of it all this wonder, stands a cottage.
I enter.
Dust hangs in the air; seeds without soil, or stars waiting in isolation for someone to draw between them the arbitrary lines that give birth to constellations.
No one is here.
No one has been here for a long time.
